(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-06"] = {"phy-12-06-m01":{"idea":"The sign between kx and ωt in a wave equation tells the direction of travel.","steps":["Follow a point of constant phase: kx − ωt = constant.","As t grows, x must grow too, to keep kx − ωt the same.","So the wave moves towards +x.","Its speed is v = ω/k."],"others":"Travel in the y direction is wrong: y is the displacement, and the wave moves along x. Travel towards −x needs a plus sign, as in kx + ωt. A stationary wave has a product of a function of x and a function of t, not a function of kx − ωt.","trap":"Minus sign: travels towards +x. Plus sign: travels towards −x.","answer":"travels in the positive x direction"},"phy-12-06-m02":{"idea":"A wave moves one wavelength in one period. This gives the basic wave relation.","steps":["The period is T = 1/n, where n is the frequency.","In one period, the wave moves forward by one wavelength λ.","Speed = distance/time = λ/T.","So v = nλ."],"others":"λ/n² has the wrong units. n/λ gives units of 1/s², not m/s. n + λ adds two quantities with different units, which makes no sense.","trap":"Check units: Hz × m = m/s.","answer":"v = nλ"},"phy-12-06-m03":{"idea":"Waves are classified by how the particles move compared with the direction of the wave.","steps":["In a transverse wave, particles move up and down.","The wave itself moves sideways, along the string.","So the vibration is at right angles to the direction of travel.","Waves on a string and light are of this type."],"others":"Gases cannot support transverse waves, so that idea is wrong. Particles do not move in circles around the source. Vibrating along the direction of travel describes a longitudinal wave, such as sound in air.","trap":"Transverse: right angles. Longitudinal: along the line of travel.","answer":"at right angles to the direction in which the wave travels"},"phy-12-06-m04":{"idea":"At a fixed end, the string cannot move, so the reflected pulse is turned upside down.","steps":["The pulse arrives at the fixed end and pulls the end up.","The wall pulls back on the string with an equal and opposite force.","This sends back a pulse in the opposite direction of displacement.","A crest returns as a trough, a phase change of π."],"others":"Upright with a phase change of π/2 is not what happens at any boundary of this kind. An absent pulse would break conservation of energy: the energy must go somewhere. Upright with no phase change is the case for a free end.","trap":"Fixed end: inverted. Free end: upright.","answer":"inverted, with a phase change of π"},"phy-12-06-m05":{"idea":"Waves add like arrows (phasors). With a phase difference of 90°, the arrows are at right angles.","steps":["A = √(A₁² + A₂² + 2A₁A₂ cos φ).","A₁ = 3 cm, A₂ = 4 cm, φ = 90°, so cos φ = 0.","A = √(9 + 16) = √25.","A = 5 cm."],"others":"7 cm is the amplitude only when the waves are in phase. 3.5 cm is just the average of the two amplitudes. 1 cm is the amplitude only when the waves are in opposite phase.","trap":"3, 4, 5 is a right triangle: this is the Pythagoras case.","answer":"5 cm"},"phy-12-06-m06":{"idea":"Intensity is proportional to the square of the amplitude. Maximum happens in phase and minimum in opposite phase.","steps":["Let A₁ = 2 and A₂ = 1.","Maximum amplitude is 2 + 1 = 3. Minimum amplitude is 2 − 1 = 1.","I_max/I_min = (3/1)².","So the ratio is 9 : 1."],"others":"5 : 3 adds and subtracts the squares instead of the amplitudes. 4 : 1 is the ratio of the intensities of the two waves. 3 : 1 is the amplitude ratio and forgets to square.","trap":"Add the amplitudes first, then square.","answer":"9 : 1"},"phy-12-06-m07":{"idea":"In a stationary wave, nodes are points of zero amplitude and they repeat at a fixed spacing.","steps":["Nodes and antinodes alternate along the string.","The displacement amplitude goes as cos(2πx/λ).","This is zero when 2πx/λ = π/2, 3π/2, and so on.","Neighbouring zeros are λ/2 apart."],"others":"2λ and λ are too large, because nodes repeat twice in every wavelength. λ/4 is the distance from a node to the next antinode, not to the next node.","trap":"Node to node: λ/2. Node to antinode: λ/4.","answer":"λ/2"},"phy-12-06-m08":{"idea":"An antinode lies exactly midway between two nodes.","steps":["The distance between consecutive nodes is λ/2.","The antinode is at the midpoint.","So the node to antinode distance is half of λ/2.","That is λ/4."],"others":"λ/2 is the node to node distance. λ is a full wavelength and spans four such gaps. λ/8 would put the antinode too close to the node.","trap":"Quarter wavelength from node to antinode.","answer":"λ/4"},"phy-12-06-m09":{"idea":"In a stationary wave, each loop moves as one piece. The amplitude changes along the loop.","steps":["The wave is y = 2a cos(2πx/λ) sin ωt.","The factor sin ωt is the same for every particle.","The factor cos(2πx/λ) has one sign inside a loop and sets the amplitude.","So the particles move in phase but with different amplitudes."],"others":"Out of phase within a single loop is wrong, since the sign of cos does not change inside it. Same amplitude is wrong, since the amplitude is largest at the antinode and zero at the nodes. Different phases within one loop does not happen.","trap":"Neighbouring loops are in opposite phase.","answer":"in phase with each other, but with different amplitudes"},"phy-12-06-m10":{"idea":"The ends of the pipe decide which standing waves can fit.","steps":["The closed end must be a node.","The open end must be an antinode.","So the pipe length holds an odd number of quarter-wavelengths: l = λ/4, 3λ/4, 5λ/4, and so on.","The frequencies are n, 3n, 5n: odd harmonics."],"others":"Even harmonics would need a node at both ends or an antinode at both ends. Only the fundamental is wrong, since higher odd modes fit too. All harmonics is the case for an open pipe, not a closed one.","trap":"Closed pipe: odd harmonics. Open pipe: all harmonics.","answer":"odd harmonics of the fundamental, n, 3n, 5n, and so on"},"phy-12-06-m11":{"idea":"An open pipe has an antinode at both ends, so it fits a half wavelength in the fundamental. A closed pipe fits only a quarter.","steps":["Open pipe: l = λ/2, so n = v/2l.","Closed pipe: l = λ/4, so n = v/4l.","Divide: n_open/n_closed = (v/2l)/(v/4l) = 2.","The open pipe has twice the frequency."],"others":"The same frequency would need the same wavelength for the same length. Half as large reverses the ratio. Four times as large mixes up the two formulas.","trap":"Closed pipe of length l sounds like an open pipe of length 2l.","answer":"twice as large"},"phy-12-06-m12":{"idea":"The overtones of a closed pipe are the odd harmonics. Count them carefully.","steps":["The harmonics are n, 3n, 5n, 7n.","The fundamental n is the first harmonic and is not an overtone.","The first overtone is 3n.","The second overtone is 5n."],"others":"3n is the first overtone, not the second. 2n is not present in a closed pipe. 4n is also an even harmonic and is not present.","trap":"First overtone is the second harmonic in an open pipe, but the third harmonic in a closed pipe.","answer":"5n"},"phy-12-06-m13":{"idea":"An open pipe has all harmonics. The nth overtone is the (n + 1)th harmonic.","steps":["The harmonics are n, 2n, 3n, 4n.","The fundamental n is not an overtone.","The first overtone is 2n.","The second overtone is 3n."],"others":"4n is the third overtone. 5n is a harmonic of a closed pipe, not the second overtone of an open pipe. 2n is the first overtone only.","trap":"Overtone number = harmonic number − 1.","answer":"3n"},"phy-12-06-m14":{"idea":"Air just outside the open end also moves, so the antinode forms slightly beyond the pipe.","steps":["An open end is an antinode, but it sits a little outside the pipe.","The extra length is the end correction e.","Experiments give e ≈ 0.3d for a pipe with inner diameter d.","A closed end is a node at the wall, so it has no end correction."],"others":"1.2 d and d/2 at the closed end are wrong, since a closed end is fixed and needs no correction. 0.6 d is the correction for both open ends of the pipe together, not for one end.","trap":"One open end: 0.3d. Two open ends: 0.6d.","answer":"0.3 d at the open end"},"phy-12-06-m15":{"idea":"The frequency of a string depends on the square root of tension.","steps":["n = (1/2l)√(T/m), so n is proportional to √T.","The tension becomes 4T.","New n = (1/2l)√(4T/m) = 2 × original.","The frequency doubles."],"others":"Half as large would need the tension to fall. Four times as large ignores the square root. √2 times as large would be right for a tension of 2T, not 4T.","trap":"Frequency goes as √T.","answer":"twice as large"},"phy-12-06-m16":{"idea":"A longer string vibrates more slowly.","steps":["n = (1/2l)√(T/m).","Tension and mass per length are the same.","So n is proportional to 1/l.","The length is doubled, so the frequency is halved."],"others":"Four times and twice as large would need a shorter string. Unchanged would need n not to depend on l, but l is in the formula.","trap":"Long string: low note. Short string: high note.","answer":"half as large"},"phy-12-06-m17":{"idea":"Two close frequencies make a loud-soft-loud pattern called beats. The beat rate is their difference.","steps":["Beat frequency = |n₁ − n₂|.","n₁ = 404 Hz and n₂ = 400 Hz.","404 − 400 = 4.","So there are 4 beats per second."],"others":"402 Hz is the average, which is the pitch you hear, not the beat rate. 804 Hz is the sum. 0.25 Hz is the time between beats in seconds, not the frequency.","trap":"Beats use the difference. The pitch heard is the average.","answer":"4 Hz"},"phy-12-06-m18":{"idea":"Beats tell us only the size of the difference. Waxing the fork helps to find the sign.","steps":["The unknown frequency is 256 ± 4, so 260 Hz or 252 Hz.","Wax adds mass, so the frequency of the fork goes down.","If it was 260 Hz, it moves towards 256 Hz and the beats decrease.","If it was 252 Hz, it moves away from 256 Hz and the beats increase. So it is 260 Hz."],"others":"264 Hz would give 8 beats, not 4. 252 Hz would give more beats after waxing, but the beats decreased. 256 Hz would give no beats at all.","trap":"Wax: lower frequency. Beats decrease means the fork was above the standard.","answer":"260 Hz"},"phy-12-06-m19":{"idea":"Resonance is the largest response of a driven system, and it needs matching frequencies.","steps":["A driven body has its own natural frequency.","If the driving frequency matches it, each push adds in step.","Energy flows in at the best rate.","The amplitude is then largest, limited only by damping."],"others":"A natural frequency of zero is meaningless. The largest driving force does not matter if the frequency does not match. A very high driving frequency gives a small response, since the body cannot keep up.","trap":"Resonance depends on frequency match, not on the strength of the push.","answer":"the frequency of the driving force is equal to the natural frequency of the body"},"phy-12-06-m20":{"idea":"In forced vibration, the driver sets the frequency. The body must follow.","steps":["The body is pushed repeatedly by the external force.","After the first transient dies out, it moves in step with the driver.","So the steady frequency is the driver's frequency.","The natural frequency only changes the size of the amplitude."],"others":"The body's own frequency appears only for free vibration. The average of the two frequencies has no physical basis. Twice the natural frequency would show up only as a special nonlinear effect.","trap":"Forced: driver's frequency. Free: natural frequency.","answer":"the frequency of the external force"},"phy-12-06-m21":{"idea":"Quality, or timbre, is what makes a violin sound different from a flute at the same pitch.","steps":["Pitch depends on the frequency of the fundamental.","Loudness depends on the amplitude.","Timbre depends on which overtones are present and how strong each one is.","Different instruments produce different mixtures of overtones."],"others":"The fundamental frequency alone sets the pitch, not the quality. Amplitude sets the loudness. The speed of sound depends on the medium and cannot tell two instruments apart.","trap":"Pitch: frequency. Loudness: intensity. Quality: overtones.","answer":"the number and relative strength of the overtones present"},"phy-12-06-m22":{"idea":"The decibel scale is logarithmic. Each factor of 10 in intensity adds 10 dB.","steps":["β = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀).","The intensity becomes 100 times larger.","Change in β = 10 log₁₀ 100.","log₁₀ 100 = 2, so the rise is 10 × 2 = 20 dB."],"others":"2 dB is just log₁₀ 100 with no factor of 10. 10 dB is the rise for a factor of 10, not 100. 100 dB treats the scale as linear.","trap":"Each factor of 10 in intensity adds 10 dB.","answer":"20 dB"},"phy-12-06-m23":{"idea":"Put the intensity into the sound level formula.","steps":["β = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀).","I/I₀ = 10⁻⁶/10⁻¹² = 10⁶.","β = 10 × log₁₀(10⁶) = 10 × 6.","β = 60 dB."],"others":"6 dB forgets the factor of 10. 600 dB multiplies by 100 instead of 10. 12 dB uses the exponent of I₀ alone.","trap":"Count the powers of ten between I and I₀, then multiply by 10.","answer":"60 dB"},"phy-12-06-m24":{"idea":"For a vibrating wire at fixed tension, a shorter wire gives a higher note.","steps":["n = (1/2l)√(T/m).","T and m are fixed, so the square root is constant.","Then n = constant/l.","So n is proportional to 1/l."],"others":"√l and l itself would make a longer wire give a higher note, which is wrong. 1/l² has too strong a dependence: the formula has l to the first power.","trap":"Sonometer rule: n × l is constant.","answer":"1/l, where l is the vibrating length"},"phy-12-06-m25":{"idea":"Put x = λ/4 into the amplitude factor.","steps":["The amplitude factor is 2a cos(2πx/λ).","At x = λ/4: 2πx/λ = π/2.","cos(π/2) = 0, so the amplitude is zero.","The point never moves, so it is a node."],"others":"Amplitude a at the midpoint would need cos(2πx/λ) = ½, which is not the case here. A node only at t = 0 is wrong, since the amplitude factor is zero for all t. An antinode needs |cos| = 1, so the amplitude would be 2a at x = 0, λ/2, λ.","trap":"Nodes: cos = 0. Antinodes: |cos| = 1.","answer":"a node, because cos(π/2) = 0"},"phy-12-06-n01":{"idea":"Wave speed is frequency times wavelength.","steps":["Use the wave speed formula v = nλ.","n = 500 Hz and λ = 0.68 m.","v = 500 × 0.68.","v = 340 m/s."],"trap":"340 m/s is the usual speed of sound in air.","answer":"340 m/s"},"phy-12-06-n02":{"idea":"Waves 90° apart add like the two sides of a right-angled triangle.","steps":["A = √(A₁² + A₂² + 2A₁A₂ cos φ).","cos 90° = 0, so A = √(A₁² + A₂²).","A = √(3² + 4²) = √25.","A = 5 cm."],"answer":"5 cm"},"phy-12-06-n03":{"idea":"Intensity goes as the square of the amplitude. Maximum is at equal phase and minimum at opposite phase.","steps":["A₁ = 3 and A₂ = 1 in some unit.","A_max = 3 + 1 = 4 and A_min = 3 − 1 = 2.","I_max/I_min = (A_max/A_min)².","= (4/2)² = 4."],"trap":"Add the amplitudes first, then square.","answer":"4"},"phy-12-06-n04":{"idea":"A closed pipe holds a quarter wavelength in its fundamental mode.","steps":["l = λ/4, so λ = 4l = 4 × 0.425 = 1.7 m.","n = v/λ.","v = 340 m/s.","n = 340/1.7 = 200 Hz."],"answer":"200 Hz"},"phy-12-06-n05":{"idea":"The end correction makes the pipe act as if it were a little longer.","steps":["e = 0.3d = 0.3 × 0.04 = 0.012 m.","Effective length = 0.50 + 0.012 = 0.512 m.","λ = 4 × 0.512 = 2.048 m.","n = v/λ = 340/2.048 = 166.0 Hz."],"trap":"Change the diameter from cm to m before using it.","answer":"166.0 Hz"},"phy-12-06-n06":{"idea":"An open pipe has all harmonics. The second overtone is the third harmonic.","steps":["Fundamental n = v/2l = 340/(2 × 0.50) = 340 Hz.","Harmonics are n, 2n, 3n.","The second overtone is the third harmonic.","3n = 3 × 340 = 1020 Hz."],"trap":"The fundamental is not an overtone, so count overtones from 2n.","answer":"1020 Hz"},"phy-12-06-n07":{"idea":"The frequency of a string fixed at both ends comes from its length, tension and mass per unit length.","steps":["n = (1/2l)√(T/m).","T/m = 200/0.005 = 40000, so √(T/m) = 200 m/s.","2l = 1.6 m.","n = 200/1.6 = 125 Hz."],"trap":"200 m/s is the wave speed on the string; n = v/2l.","answer":"125 Hz"},"phy-12-06-n08":{"idea":"The beat frequency is the difference of the two frequencies. The time between beats is its inverse.","steps":["Beat frequency = 512 − 508 = 4 Hz.","4 loud sounds are heard each second.","Time between them = 1/4 s.","= 0.25 s."],"answer":"0.25 s"},"phy-12-06-n09":{"idea":"Use the decibel formula with the given intensity.","steps":["β = 10 log₁₀(I/I₀).","I/I₀ = 2 × 10⁻⁷/10⁻¹² = 2 × 10⁵.","log₁₀(2 × 10⁵) = 5 + 0.301 = 5.301.","β = 10 × 5.301 = 53.01 dB."],"answer":"53.01 dB"},"phy-12-06-n10":{"idea":"Intensities add. Decibel levels do not add.","steps":["60 dB means I = 10⁻⁶ W/m² for one source, since 10 log₁₀(I/10⁻¹²) = 60.","Two sources give I = 2 × 10⁻⁶ W/m².","β = 10 log₁₀(2 × 10⁶) = 10 × (6 + 0.301).","β = 63.01 dB."],"trap":"Doubling the intensity adds about 3 dB, never 60 dB.","answer":"63.01 dB"},"phy-12-06-n11":{"idea":"In a stationary wave with an antinode at x = 0, the nodes are at odd multiples of a quarter wavelength.","steps":["Nodes are at x = λ/4, 3λ/4, 5λ/4, and so on.","The third node is at 5λ/4.","λ = 0.60 m.","x = 5 × 0.60/4 = 0.75 m."],"trap":"Check: the nodes are at 0.15, 0.45 and 0.75 m, each 0.30 m apart (λ/2).","answer":"0.75 m"}};
